<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                      http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd"
    objectQuotingStrategy="QUOTE_ONLY_RESERVED_WORDS">

    <!-- How long the index build may wait for a lock; a Liquibase parameter of the same name overrides it. -->
    <property name="assignmentTeamIndexLockTimeout" value="30s"/>
    <!-- Set, change or remove that override only for the start that runs the changeset: Liquibase expands the
         parameter into the SQL before it computes the checksum, so once the changeset ran, an override set,
         changed or removed makes every later start fail validation (release-boundary.md, Release). -->

    <!--
      Assignments by team. The phone syncs and the supervisor scope read a team's assignments, and no index starts
      with team_id: it is the third column of uc_assignment_activity_id. So the org-unit sync (findAllByTeamUidIn)
      read the whole table, and the assignment sync (AssignmentFilter) and the supervisor scope
      (findManagedScope) walked the whole of that 25-27 MB index once per team. Production counted 3,453
      sequential scans of assignment reading 333 M rows by 2026-11-24 21:37 UTC (233 k rows, 75 MB).

      Measured on staging (a production clone), EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) for the three busiest teams and for the
      supervisor team that manages the most teams (205), then the index built and the table analysed in the same
      transaction, rolled back:
      - assignment sync, own teams: 408.6 ms and 23,878 buffers, then 12.2 ms and 606;
      - org-unit sync: 88.6 ms and 8,136 buffers (a sequential scan), then 5.6 ms and 594;
      - supervisor scope: 3,276.5 ms and 662,789 buffers, then 28.2 ms and 8,319.
      The build took 0.3 s and made a 2 MB index.

      A plain CREATE INDEX in the changeset's transaction: it takes a SHARE lock, which blocks writes to assignment
      (admin configuration only) for well under a second and no reader. lock_timeout makes the deploy fail visibly,
      instead of waiting, if a write holds the table. It runs while no valid index of that name exists, and marks
      itself ran otherwise; an invalid leftover is dropped and the index built normally.

      Rollback drops the index; nothing depends on it but query speed.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20261125-02-assignment-team-index" author="datarun">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                SELECT count(*)
                FROM pg_index i
                JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.indexrelid
                WHERE c.relname = 'idx_assignment_team_id' AND i.indisvalid
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <sql>SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '${assignmentTeamIndexLockTimeout}'</sql>
        <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_assignment_team_id</sql>
        <sql>CREATE INDEX idx_assignment_team_id ON assignment (team_id)</sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '${assignmentTeamIndexLockTimeout}'</sql>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_assignment_team_id</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
